1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,879
Hello, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:09,879 --> 00:00:17,006
This is a place for slow stories from the past, told softly, so that you can rest while you listen.

3
00:00:17,006 --> 00:00:23,196
There is nothing you need to remember tonight, and nothing you need to follow closely.

4
00:00:23,196 --> 00:00:28,523
If you drift away halfway through a sentence, that is perfectly all right.

5
00:00:28,523 --> 00:00:35,074
The story will carry on quietly without you, and it will be here again whenever you return.

6
00:00:36,374 --> 00:00:39,222
Before we begin, let yourself settle.

7
00:00:39,222 --> 00:00:43,148
Find the position that feels easiest for your body.

8
00:00:43,148 --> 00:00:48,306
Let your shoulders fall a little lower than they were a moment ago.

9
00:00:48,306 --> 00:00:51,924
Let your hands rest wherever they want to rest.

10
00:00:51,924 --> 00:00:56,619
And take one slow breath in, and let it go, without hurrying.

11
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:02,258
Tonight, we are going to spend an evening in ancient Rome.

12
00:01:02,258 --> 00:01:10,786
Not the Rome of battles and emperors and great speeches, but the quieter city that existed underneath all of that.

13
00:01:10,786 --> 00:01:14,003
The Rome of lamps being lit in small rooms.

14
00:01:14,003 --> 00:01:17,070
Of carts rolling in from the countryside.

15
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:21,783
Of priestesses tending a fire that was never allowed to go out.

16
00:01:21,783 --> 00:01:26,272
Of ships resting in a harbour, waiting for the morning wind.

17
00:01:27,572 --> 00:01:35,113
We will arrive the way many travellers once arrived, along an old stone road, just as the light is fading.

18
00:01:35,113 --> 00:01:39,453
We will walk into the city as it changes from day into night.

19
00:01:39,453 --> 00:01:49,342
We will pass through the great public square, now mostly empty, and step inside an ordinary home where a family is getting ready for sleep.

20
00:01:49,342 --> 00:01:58,164
Later, we will drift down to the sea, and finally out into the soft hills of the countryside, where the night is very still.

21
00:01:59,463 --> 00:02:07,018
So let the noise of your own day fall behind you now, the way a road falls behind a traveller.

22
00:02:07,018 --> 00:02:11,760
The sky over the hills is turning the colour of old copper.

23
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,135
The first stars are appearing, one by one.

24
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:21,565
And somewhere ahead of us, in the warm evening air, the city of Rome is waiting.

25
00:02:30,865 --> 00:02:34,327
The road beneath us is called the Appian Way.

26
00:02:34,327 --> 00:02:41,789
It is one of the oldest of all the great Roman roads, and the Romans themselves were proud of it.

27
00:02:41,789 --> 00:02:49,636
A poet once called it the queen of roads, and walking along it in the evening, you can understand why.

28
00:02:49,636 --> 00:02:56,252
It runs straight and patient across the land, as if it has never once been in a hurry.

29
00:02:57,551 --> 00:03:06,878
The road was begun more than three hundred years before the time of the emperors, by a Roman official named Appius Claudius.

30
00:03:06,878 --> 00:03:09,435
That is where its name comes from.

31
00:03:09,435 --> 00:03:13,422
At first it ran south from Rome to the city of Capua.

32
00:03:13,422 --> 00:03:26,885
Over the generations it was extended, mile by mile, until it reached all the way to the port of Brundisium, on the heel of Italy, where ships set out across the sea toward Greece.

33
00:03:28,185 --> 00:03:35,480
The surface is made of large, flat stones, fitted closely together like the pieces of a puzzle.

34
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:41,776
They are dark and smooth, worn down by centuries of wheels and hooves and sandals.

35
00:03:41,776 --> 00:03:46,844
In the fading light, the stones hold a little of the day's warmth.

36
00:03:46,844 --> 00:03:53,678
If you were to rest your hand on one, it would feel faintly warm, like a sleeping animal.

37
00:03:54,978 --> 00:04:00,182
Every so often, at the side of the road, there is a tall stone pillar.

38
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:01,743
These are milestones.

39
00:04:01,743 --> 00:04:08,508
A Roman mile was a thousand paces, counted as a thousand double steps of a walking soldier.

40
00:04:08,508 --> 00:04:15,347
Each milestone tells the traveller how far they have come, or how far they still have to go.

41
00:04:15,347 --> 00:04:18,172
Tonight, we do not need to count them.

42
00:04:18,172 --> 00:04:24,120
We can simply let them pass, one after another, like slow beats of a quiet song.

43
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:28,415
On both sides of the road there are tombs.

44
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:32,979
That might sound strange at first, but it is not a gloomy sight.

45
00:04:32,979 --> 00:04:46,814
Roman law did not allow the dead to be buried inside the city walls, and so families built their monuments along the roads leading out of town, where travellers would pass by and read the names.

46
00:04:46,814 --> 00:04:49,025
Some tombs are small and plain.

47
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:57,654
Others are grand, round towers of stone, with carved inscriptions asking the passerby to pause for a moment and remember.

48
00:04:58,954 --> 00:05:12,685
Between the tombs grow tall umbrella pines, their flat green tops spread out against the evening sky, and narrow dark cypress trees, pointing upward like quiet candles.

49
00:05:12,685 --> 00:05:22,655
The air smells of warm resin, of dust settling after a long day, and faintly, of wood smoke drifting from somewhere ahead.

50
00:05:23,955 --> 00:05:27,782
There are still a few other travellers on the road.

51
00:05:27,782 --> 00:05:35,210
A farmer walks beside a mule loaded with baskets of vegetables, hoping to sell them in the morning.

52
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:40,913
A small group of pilgrims talk in low voices, tired from walking since dawn.

53
00:05:40,913 --> 00:05:52,393
A messenger rides past on horseback, a little faster than everyone else, and then the sound of his horse fades away down the road, and it is quiet again.

54
00:05:53,693 --> 00:06:04,185
Along the way there are inns and resting places, where travellers can water their animals and find a simple meal and a bed for the night.

55
00:06:04,185 --> 00:06:05,794
Some are respectable.

56
00:06:05,794 --> 00:06:07,632
Some are a little rough.

57
00:06:07,632 --> 00:06:18,354
But at this hour, most of them glow softly from within, and the murmur of conversation drifts out through their doorways into the warm dusk.

58
00:06:19,654 --> 00:06:23,872
Ahead of us, the walls of the city are coming into view.

59
00:06:23,872 --> 00:06:28,240
The road leads toward an old gate called the Porta Capena.

60
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:32,382
From here, the Appian Way began its long journey south.

61
00:06:32,382 --> 00:06:43,227
And from here, tonight, our journey turns the other way, into the heart of the city, as the last of the daylight slips quietly behind the hills.

62
00:06:52,527 --> 00:06:54,869
Rome at this time was enormous.

63
00:06:54,869 --> 00:07:02,572
Historians often estimate that, at its height, the city may have been home to around a million people.

64
00:07:02,572 --> 00:07:06,348
That is a remarkable number for the ancient world.

65
00:07:06,348 --> 00:07:20,773
Imagine all of those lives, folded together into narrow streets and crowded hillsides, each one with its own small worries, its own small comforts, its own way of getting ready for the night.

66
00:07:22,073 --> 00:07:25,845
Most ordinary Romans did not live in grand houses.

67
00:07:25,845 --> 00:07:39,575
They lived in tall apartment buildings that the Romans called insulae, which means islands, perhaps because each block stood surrounded by streets like an island surrounded by water.

68
00:07:39,575 --> 00:07:46,440
On the ground floor there were often shops and workshops, opening directly onto the street.

69
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:51,193
Above them, floor after floor, were rooms for families to rent.

70
00:07:52,493 --> 00:07:59,075
In these buildings, the lower floors were usually the most comfortable and the most expensive.

71
00:07:59,075 --> 00:08:03,627
The higher you climbed, the smaller and cheaper the rooms became.

72
00:08:03,627 --> 00:08:09,649
Up near the roof, the rooms were cramped, and hot in summer, and the stairs were long.

73
00:08:09,649 --> 00:08:19,522
But from those top windows, on a clear night, you could see across the rooftops of the whole city, all the way to the dark line of the hills.

74
00:08:20,822 --> 00:08:29,392
The buildings grew so tall that the emperor Augustus set a limit on their height, of about seventy Roman feet.

75
00:08:29,392 --> 00:08:33,989
Later, the emperor Trajan lowered that limit a little more.

76
00:08:33,989 --> 00:08:47,467
Even so, from street level, the insulae must have seemed to rise up on either side like the walls of a canyon, with only a narrow ribbon of evening sky showing between them.

77
00:08:48,768 --> 00:08:52,748
Now, as the light fades, the city changes its rhythm.

78
00:08:52,748 --> 00:08:58,004
The shopkeepers pull wooden shutters across the fronts of their shops.

79
00:08:58,004 --> 00:09:00,933
A baker sweeps flour from his doorstep.

80
00:09:00,933 --> 00:09:04,387
A woman calls her children in from the street.

81
00:09:04,387 --> 00:09:14,901
Someone, high up in one of the buildings, is singing softly while they work, and the sound floats down into the street and then drifts away.

82
00:09:16,201 --> 00:09:20,708
There is something you might not expect about the Roman night.

83
00:09:20,708 --> 00:09:26,378
During the daytime, most wheeled carts were not allowed into the city streets.

84
00:09:26,378 --> 00:09:29,722
The roads were simply too crowded with people.

85
00:09:29,722 --> 00:09:38,300
So the heavy wagons, carrying stone and timber and goods from the countryside, waited outside the gates until evening.

86
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:41,353
And when the evening came, they rolled in.

87
00:09:42,653 --> 00:09:47,143
So in many neighbourhoods, the night was not completely silent.

88
00:09:47,143 --> 00:09:58,760
There was the slow rumble of wheels over stone, the creak of wooden axles, the patient breathing of oxen, the voices of drivers calling to one another in the dark.

89
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:04,319
One Roman poet complained that it was impossible to get any sleep in the city.

90
00:10:04,319 --> 00:10:05,816
Perhaps he was right.

91
00:10:05,816 --> 00:10:14,440
But from a distance, from up on a hill, those sounds blend together into a low, steady murmur, like the sound of the sea.

92
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:20,070
Moving quietly through the streets, there are also the watchmen.

93
00:10:20,070 --> 00:10:29,273
They were called the vigiles, and the emperor Augustus organised them into seven companies, each one responsible for a part of the city.

94
00:10:29,273 --> 00:10:36,717
Their main task was to watch for fires, which were a constant danger in a city of wooden floors and oil lamps.

95
00:10:36,717 --> 00:10:47,611
They carried buckets and ropes and axes, and they walked slowly through the dark streets, looking up at the windows, listening, keeping watch while others slept.

96
00:10:48,912 --> 00:10:52,444
Picture one of them now, pausing at a corner.

97
00:10:52,444 --> 00:10:57,469
He looks up and sees a small light in a window, three floors up.

98
00:10:57,469 --> 00:11:03,749
Someone is still awake, perhaps reading, perhaps mending something by lamplight.

99
00:11:03,749 --> 00:11:05,633
He watches for a moment.

100
00:11:05,633 --> 00:11:07,910
The flame is steady and calm.

101
00:11:07,910 --> 00:11:10,501
He nods to himself, and walks on.

102
00:11:11,801 --> 00:11:15,349
Above it all, the moon has risen over the rooftops.

103
00:11:15,349 --> 00:11:18,201
It turns the tiled roofs pale and silver.

104
00:11:18,201 --> 00:11:30,374
It catches on the rounded shapes of domes, on the tops of columns, on the long arches of the aqueducts that carry fresh water into the city from springs far away in the hills.

105
00:11:30,374 --> 00:11:44,078
The water flows through those channels all night long, quietly, without anyone needing to watch it, filling the public fountains so that in the morning there will be fresh, cool water for everyone.

106
00:11:53,378 --> 00:11:59,541
Before the city sleeps completely, let us pass by one of the great public baths.

107
00:11:59,541 --> 00:12:04,240
For the Romans, the baths were not only a place to get clean.

108
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:12,020
They were a place to meet friends, to exercise, to talk, to relax, and to simply spend the afternoon.

109
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:20,109
Even people of very modest means could go, because the entrance fee was tiny, just the smallest of coins.

110
00:12:21,409 --> 00:12:27,046
The baths usually opened in the early afternoon and closed around sunset.

111
00:12:27,046 --> 00:12:31,294
So now, as evening falls, the last bathers are leaving.

112
00:12:31,294 --> 00:12:44,577
They step out through the great doors into the cooler air, their skin warm and soft, their hair still damp, talking quietly as they walk home through the darkening streets.

113
00:12:45,877 --> 00:12:50,421
A Roman bath was a journey through rooms of different temperatures.

114
00:12:50,421 --> 00:12:56,525
First there was a changing room, with niches in the walls where people left their clothes.

115
00:12:56,525 --> 00:13:02,901
Then there was a warm room, gently heated, where you could sit and let your body slowly relax.

116
00:13:02,901 --> 00:13:08,124
Then a hot room, full of steam, where the air was thick and heavy and sleepy.

117
00:13:08,124 --> 00:13:15,381
And finally, a cold room with a deep pool, where you could plunge into cool water and come out feeling new.

118
00:13:16,681 --> 00:13:19,376
The rooms were heated in a clever way.

119
00:13:19,376 --> 00:13:25,477
The floors were raised up on small stacks of tiles, leaving a hollow space underneath.

120
00:13:25,477 --> 00:13:39,806
Furnaces at the side of the building pushed hot air through that space, so that the floors themselves grew warm, and in the hottest rooms, so warm that bathers wore wooden sandals to protect their feet.

121
00:13:39,806 --> 00:13:45,694
The warmth rose up through the floor and the walls and filled the rooms from below.

122
00:13:46,994 --> 00:13:49,934
Romans did not use soap the way we do.

123
00:13:49,934 --> 00:14:02,388
Instead, they rubbed olive oil into their skin, and then scraped it away with a curved metal tool called a strigil, taking the dust and sweat of the day with it.

124
00:14:02,388 --> 00:14:13,682
Then they rinsed, and rested, and perhaps had a massage, or simply lay quietly in the warmth, listening to the echo of water in the vaulted halls.

125
00:14:14,982 --> 00:14:17,726
Now, though, the halls are emptying.

126
00:14:17,726 --> 00:14:25,729
The attendants walk slowly through the rooms, gathering forgotten towels, wiping down the marble benches.

127
00:14:25,729 --> 00:14:27,786
The great pools grow still.

128
00:14:27,786 --> 00:14:34,798
Their surfaces become perfectly smooth, reflecting the high ceilings and the last few lamps.

129
00:14:34,798 --> 00:14:42,267
The echoes fade, one by one, until the only sound is the slow drip of water somewhere in the dark.

130
00:14:43,567 --> 00:14:50,018
Down below, beside the furnaces, the stokers let the fires burn lower for the night.

131
00:14:50,018 --> 00:14:58,387
The heat will linger in the stones for hours, slowly fading, like the warmth of a bed after you have left it.

132
00:14:58,387 --> 00:15:05,682
Tomorrow afternoon, the fires will be fed again, and the halls will fill with voices and steam.

133
00:15:05,682 --> 00:15:08,677
But tonight, the baths are resting too.

134
00:15:17,977 --> 00:15:22,751
Let us walk now toward the centre of the city, to the Forum.

135
00:15:22,751 --> 00:15:26,809
During the day, this was the busiest place in Rome.

136
00:15:26,809 --> 00:15:41,849
It was a great open space, surrounded by temples and law courts and meeting halls, filled with people arguing, buying, selling, listening to speeches, and simply watching one another go by.

137
00:15:43,149 --> 00:15:47,162
But at night, the Forum is a very different place.

138
00:15:47,162 --> 00:15:49,249
The crowds have gone home.

139
00:15:49,249 --> 00:15:51,015
The voices have faded.

140
00:15:51,015 --> 00:15:59,603
The tall columns stand in long, quiet rows, casting soft shadows across the paving stones in the moonlight.

141
00:15:59,603 --> 00:16:08,834
Here and there a cat moves silently across the open ground, and disappears into the darkness between two buildings.

142
00:16:10,134 --> 00:16:15,391
At one end of the Forum stands the speakers' platform, called the Rostra.

143
00:16:15,391 --> 00:16:20,576
During the day, famous orators stood here and spoke to the crowds below.

144
00:16:20,576 --> 00:16:21,729
Now it is empty.

145
00:16:21,729 --> 00:16:29,290
If you stood on it tonight, there would be no one to hear you, and perhaps that would feel like a relief.

146
00:16:29,290 --> 00:16:31,955
Some places are made for many voices.

147
00:16:31,955 --> 00:16:36,203
But they are also beautiful when they are completely still.

148
00:16:37,503 --> 00:16:41,347
Not far away stands the Senate house, the Curia.

149
00:16:41,347 --> 00:16:45,750
It is a tall, simple building, with heavy bronze doors.

150
00:16:45,750 --> 00:16:53,436
Inside, during the day, the senators sat on long benches and debated the business of the empire.

151
00:16:53,436 --> 00:17:04,405
Tonight the doors are closed, and the great room inside is dark and cool and silent, holding all of those old words quietly in its walls.

152
00:17:05,705 --> 00:17:13,632
Near the temple of Saturn, there is a small monument that the Romans called the Golden Milestone.

153
00:17:13,632 --> 00:17:26,463
It was set up by the emperor Augustus, and it was said to mark the symbolic centre of the Roman road network, the place from which all the great roads began.

154
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:30,631
There is an old saying that all roads lead to Rome.

155
00:17:30,631 --> 00:17:48,365
Standing here, in the quiet of the night, it is pleasant to imagine all of those roads, stretching away in every direction across the dark countryside, and all of them, eventually, leading back to this one small spot.

156
00:17:49,665 --> 00:17:57,075
And now, at the edge of the Forum, there is a small round temple, glowing softly from within.

157
00:17:57,075 --> 00:18:02,492
This is the temple of Vesta, the goddess of the hearth and the home.

158
00:18:02,492 --> 00:18:07,671
It is one of the oldest and most sacred places in the whole city.

159
00:18:08,971 --> 00:18:14,607
Inside this temple burns a fire, and that fire is never allowed to go out.

160
00:18:14,607 --> 00:18:22,680
The Romans believed that as long as the sacred flame of Vesta kept burning, the city itself would be safe.

161
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:29,154
The fire was looked after by a small group of priestesses, called the Vestal Virgins.

162
00:18:29,154 --> 00:18:35,552
There were only six of them at a time, and they served the goddess for thirty years.

163
00:18:36,852 --> 00:18:40,043
Imagine one of them now, at this late hour.

164
00:18:40,043 --> 00:18:47,982
She moves quietly around the hearth, adding a little wood to the fire, watching the flames settle and glow.

165
00:18:47,982 --> 00:18:51,395
The light flickers gently on the curved walls.

166
00:18:51,395 --> 00:19:04,009
Outside, the whole city is falling asleep, but here, in this small round room, someone is awake, keeping the flame alive, as it has been kept alive for hundreds of years.

167
00:19:05,309 --> 00:19:09,021
There is something very calming about that idea.

168
00:19:09,021 --> 00:19:14,279
Somewhere, someone is watching over the fire, so you do not have to.

169
00:19:14,279 --> 00:19:18,532
You can let go of whatever you were holding onto today.

170
00:19:18,532 --> 00:19:21,470
Someone else is keeping watch tonight.

171
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:22,862
The flame is safe.

172
00:19:22,862 --> 00:19:24,176
The city is safe.

173
00:19:24,176 --> 00:19:25,491
And you can rest.

174
00:19:26,791 --> 00:19:31,034
The moon moves slowly across the sky above the Forum.

175
00:19:31,034 --> 00:19:36,799
The shadows of the columns shift, a little at a time, across the stones.

176
00:19:36,799 --> 00:19:46,887
A breeze moves through the open square, carrying the faint scent of the fire from the temple, and then that, too, grows still.

177
00:19:56,187 --> 00:20:00,857
Let us leave the open spaces now, and step inside a home.

178
00:20:00,857 --> 00:20:07,085
Not a palace, but a comfortable house belonging to a family of modest means.

179
00:20:07,085 --> 00:20:16,261
The street door is heavy and wooden, and once it closes behind us, the sounds of the city grow soft and distant.

180
00:20:17,561 --> 00:20:22,157
Roman houses of this kind often looked inward rather than outward.

181
00:20:22,157 --> 00:20:26,613
From the street, they showed only plain walls and a single door.

182
00:20:26,613 --> 00:20:35,317
But inside, the rooms were arranged around an open hall called the atrium, with a gap in the roof that let in light and rain.

183
00:20:35,317 --> 00:20:41,235
Beneath that opening, in the floor, there was a shallow basin to catch the rainwater.

184
00:20:41,235 --> 00:20:50,287
Tonight, the moon is shining through the opening in the roof, and its reflection lies perfectly still on the surface of the water.

185
00:20:51,587 --> 00:20:58,000
The main meal of the day, the cena, was eaten in the late afternoon or early evening.

186
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,792
The family has already eaten tonight.

187
00:21:00,792 --> 00:21:08,790
There was bread, and olives, and lentils cooked with herbs, and a little cheese, and some fruit to finish.

188
00:21:08,790 --> 00:21:11,356
The dishes have been cleared away.

189
00:21:11,356 --> 00:21:17,165
In the kitchen, the fire in the hearth has burned down to a soft orange glow.

190
00:21:18,465 --> 00:21:21,291
Now the house is lit by small oil lamps.

191
00:21:21,291 --> 00:21:31,392
These were made of clay, or sometimes of bronze, shaped a little like a flattened teapot, with a spout at one end where a wick of linen rested.

192
00:21:31,392 --> 00:21:36,690
They were filled with olive oil, and they gave a small, warm, steady flame.

193
00:21:36,690 --> 00:21:40,080
It was not a bright light, not by our standards.

194
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:45,943
But it was enough to read by, enough to see a face, enough to find your way to bed.

195
00:21:47,243 --> 00:21:53,663
In a small room off the atrium, the father of the family is sitting at a table with a scroll.

196
00:21:53,663 --> 00:22:02,292
Books in Rome were not bound pages but long rolls, which you unrolled with one hand and rolled up with the other as you read.

197
00:22:02,292 --> 00:22:08,712
Romans often read aloud, even when they were alone, softly murmuring the words to themselves.

198
00:22:08,712 --> 00:22:17,963
So if you listen carefully, you can hear him now, reading a few lines of poetry in a low voice, slowly, as if he is tasting each word.

199
00:22:19,263 --> 00:22:23,062
The Romans counted time differently from the way we do.

200
00:22:23,062 --> 00:22:28,243
They divided the daylight into twelve hours, and the darkness into watches.

201
00:22:28,243 --> 00:22:35,220
This meant that a summer hour was longer than a winter hour, because the days themselves were longer.

202
00:22:35,220 --> 00:22:39,779
Time stretched and shrank with the seasons, like something living.

203
00:22:39,779 --> 00:22:43,163
At night, there was no clock ticking on the wall.

204
00:22:43,163 --> 00:22:49,449
There was only the slow movement of the stars, and the oil slowly burning down in the lamp.

205
00:22:50,749 --> 00:22:55,244
Elsewhere in the house, the children have already gone to bed.

206
00:22:55,244 --> 00:22:59,231
Roman bedrooms, called cubicula, were small and simple.

207
00:22:59,231 --> 00:23:09,307
Often there was just a narrow bed, a wooden frame with a mattress stuffed with wool or straw, a blanket, perhaps a small chest for clothes.

208
00:23:09,307 --> 00:23:12,931
These rooms were not for living in during the day.

209
00:23:12,931 --> 00:23:18,440
They were only for sleeping, and so they were kept dark and quiet and close.

210
00:23:19,740 --> 00:23:26,675
Most Romans went to bed early, not long after dark, and rose with the first light of morning.

211
00:23:26,675 --> 00:23:29,732
There was a kind of natural rhythm to it.

212
00:23:29,732 --> 00:23:33,982
When the sun went down, the work of the day was finished.

213
00:23:33,982 --> 00:23:38,456
When the lamps were blown out, the house settled into sleep.

214
00:23:38,456 --> 00:23:43,228
And when the sky began to grey in the east, the day began again.

215
00:23:44,528 --> 00:23:49,379
In the kitchen, an old servant makes one last round of the house.

216
00:23:49,379 --> 00:23:53,559
She checks that the fire in the hearth is safely banked.

217
00:23:53,559 --> 00:23:56,917
She closes a shutter that has been left open.

218
00:23:56,917 --> 00:24:04,754
She pauses in the atrium and looks up at the square of moonlit sky in the roof, and listens to the quiet.

219
00:24:04,754 --> 00:24:09,232
Somewhere in the house, someone sighs softly in their sleep.

220
00:24:10,532 --> 00:24:15,426
Back in the study, the father has come to the end of his reading.

221
00:24:15,426 --> 00:24:20,620
He rolls the scroll up slowly and carefully, and ties it with a cord.

222
00:24:20,620 --> 00:24:26,793
He sits for a moment in the soft light, not thinking about anything in particular.

223
00:24:26,793 --> 00:24:30,783
Then he leans forward, and gently blows out the lamp.

224
00:24:30,783 --> 00:24:34,849
A thin line of smoke rises in the dark and curls away.

225
00:24:34,849 --> 00:24:36,580
And the house is still.

226
00:24:45,880 --> 00:24:51,746
Let us step outside again, into the cool night, and walk down toward the river.

227
00:24:51,746 --> 00:24:59,319
The Tiber flows through the heart of Rome, wide and slow, curving gently around the base of the hills.

228
00:24:59,319 --> 00:25:08,451
It is the reason the city grew here in the first place, where the river could be crossed and where boats could come and go.

229
00:25:09,751 --> 00:25:16,348
In the middle of the river there is a small island, long and narrow, with pointed ends.

230
00:25:16,348 --> 00:25:32,044
The Romans said it looked like a ship, and at some point they even faced its stone edges to look like the bow of a boat, as if the whole island were sailing slowly down the river and never arriving anywhere.

231
00:25:33,344 --> 00:25:37,731
On that island stood a temple to Aesculapius, the god of healing.

232
00:25:37,731 --> 00:25:45,963
The story goes that, long ago, during a time of sickness, the Romans sent a ship to Greece to bring the god to their city.

233
00:25:45,963 --> 00:25:51,362
On the way home, a sacred serpent slipped from the ship and swam to this island.

234
00:25:51,362 --> 00:25:55,208
The Romans took it as a sign, and built the temple there.

235
00:25:55,208 --> 00:26:06,005
For centuries afterward, people who were unwell came to the island to rest and to recover, sleeping in the quiet halls of the temple, hoping for healing dreams.

236
00:26:07,305 --> 00:26:10,919
There is something gentle in that idea, too.

237
00:26:10,919 --> 00:26:15,436
An island in the middle of a river, set aside for rest.

238
00:26:15,436 --> 00:26:22,580
A place where people came simply to lie down, and sleep, and let time do its slow work.

239
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:30,492
Crossing from the city to the island is a stone bridge with two wide, graceful arches.

240
00:26:30,492 --> 00:26:41,102
It was built in the last years before the emperors, and it is so well made that it still stands today, more than two thousand years later.

241
00:26:41,102 --> 00:26:43,255
People still walk across it.

242
00:26:43,255 --> 00:26:51,635
If you were to visit Rome now, you could stand on the very same stones, and look down at the very same river.

243
00:26:52,935 --> 00:26:55,312
Tonight, the bridge is empty.

244
00:26:55,312 --> 00:27:10,065
The moonlight lies along its curved arches and their reflections in the water below, so that each arch forms a perfect circle, half made of stone, and half made of water and light.

245
00:27:11,365 --> 00:27:20,042
Along the riverbank, small boats are tied up for the night, bumping softly against one another as the current moves past.

246
00:27:20,042 --> 00:27:28,718
A fisherman sits on the bank with a line in the water, not really expecting to catch anything, simply enjoying the quiet.

247
00:27:28,718 --> 00:27:34,383
After a while, he winds in his line, gathers his things, and walks slowly home.

248
00:27:34,383 --> 00:27:41,339
And the river flows on alone, past the island, past the bridges, out of the city, toward the sea.

249
00:27:50,639 --> 00:28:00,348
Let us follow the river now, as it winds its way westward, out of the city and across the flat green land toward the coast.

250
00:28:00,348 --> 00:28:04,136
Beyond the walls, the banks grow wide and empty.

251
00:28:04,136 --> 00:28:14,319
Reeds rustle softly at the water's edge, and moonlight lies on the river in long, broken ribbons, moving gently with the current.

252
00:28:15,619 --> 00:28:21,287
At the mouth of the river lay the town of Ostia, the harbour of Rome.

253
00:28:21,287 --> 00:28:28,598
A city of a million people needed an enormous amount of food, and much of it came by sea.

254
00:28:28,598 --> 00:28:38,701
Ships arrived here from all across the Mediterranean, carrying wine and olive oil, timber and marble, and above all, grain.

255
00:28:40,001 --> 00:28:50,518
Much of that grain came from Egypt, where the river Nile flooded every year and left behind rich, dark soil that grew wheat in great abundance.

256
00:28:50,518 --> 00:28:55,151
Large merchant ships carried the grain across the sea to Italy.

257
00:28:55,151 --> 00:29:03,241
These were wide, round-bellied ships with a great square sail, and they were slow and steady rather than fast.

258
00:29:03,241 --> 00:29:08,609
When they appeared on the horizon, people knew that bread was on its way.

259
00:29:09,909 --> 00:29:16,334
Over time the harbour at Ostia became too small and too shallow for so many ships.

260
00:29:16,334 --> 00:29:26,440
So the emperor Claudius began building a great new harbour a little to the north, with long stone arms reaching out into the sea.

261
00:29:26,440 --> 00:29:36,939
Later, the emperor Trajan added an inner basin, shaped like a hexagon, where ships could rest in calm water, protected from the waves.

262
00:29:38,239 --> 00:29:42,426
At the entrance to Claudius' harbour there stood a lighthouse.

263
00:29:42,426 --> 00:29:46,614
And there is a lovely story about how its foundation was made.

264
00:29:46,614 --> 00:29:53,841
A huge ship had once been built to carry an obelisk, a tall stone monument, all the way from Egypt to Rome.

265
00:29:53,841 --> 00:30:02,013
When its work was done, that ship was filled with stone and sunk into the sea, and the lighthouse was built on top of it.

266
00:30:02,013 --> 00:30:09,983
So the light that guided sailors home at night stood on the back of an old ship, resting at the bottom of the harbour.

267
00:30:11,283 --> 00:30:16,121
Tonight, the lighthouse glows at the edge of the dark water.

268
00:30:16,121 --> 00:30:21,362
Its fire is small at this distance, a single warm point of light.

269
00:30:21,362 --> 00:30:30,070
But to a sailor out at sea, tired from many days of travel, it would be the most welcome sight in the world.

270
00:30:30,070 --> 00:30:33,296
It means the long voyage is nearly over.

271
00:30:33,296 --> 00:30:35,069
It means rest is near.

272
00:30:36,369 --> 00:30:40,464
In the harbour, the ships are anchored for the night.

273
00:30:40,464 --> 00:30:42,241
Their sails are furled.

274
00:30:42,241 --> 00:30:54,835
The water laps softly against their wooden sides, and their masts sway very slowly, back and forth, back and forth, as the gentle swell rolls in from the open sea.

275
00:30:54,835 --> 00:31:01,479
On board, most of the sailors are already asleep, rocked by the movement of the water.

276
00:31:02,779 --> 00:31:10,189
Along the quays stand great warehouses, called horrea, where the grain and other goods were stored.

277
00:31:10,189 --> 00:31:16,401
They are long, solid buildings, with thick walls to keep out the damp and the heat.

278
00:31:16,401 --> 00:31:28,976
Inside, in the dark, the sacks and jars stand in rows, waiting patiently for morning, when they will be loaded onto river barges and pulled slowly up the Tiber to Rome.

279
00:31:30,276 --> 00:31:39,369
Those barges were often towed upstream by teams of oxen walking along the riverbank, or by men pulling on long ropes.

280
00:31:39,369 --> 00:31:41,312
It was slow, steady work.

281
00:31:41,312 --> 00:31:54,212
But tonight, the oxen are resting in their stalls, and the ropes are coiled on the quays, and the river flows on without them, carrying the moonlight down to the sea.

282
00:31:55,512 --> 00:31:59,194
Stand here for a moment at the edge of the water.

283
00:31:59,194 --> 00:32:02,500
Feel the cool breeze coming in from the sea.

284
00:32:02,500 --> 00:32:07,084
Listen to the waves, rolling in, and slipping back out again.

285
00:32:07,084 --> 00:32:07,986
In, and out.

286
00:32:07,986 --> 00:32:08,887
In, and out.

287
00:32:08,887 --> 00:32:10,390
Like slow breathing.

288
00:32:10,390 --> 00:32:20,835
The sea has been breathing like this for longer than anyone can remember, and it will go on breathing like this long after tonight is over.

289
00:32:30,135 --> 00:32:40,345
Now let us leave the coast behind, and drift inland, over fields and olive groves, into the soft hills of the countryside.

290
00:32:40,345 --> 00:32:46,455
Here, away from the city and the harbour, the night is very quiet indeed.

291
00:32:47,755 --> 00:32:52,789
Many wealthy Romans owned houses in the country, called villas.

292
00:32:52,789 --> 00:32:58,621
Some were working farms, with vineyards and orchards and fields of grain.

293
00:32:58,621 --> 00:33:09,088
Others were places of retreat, where people went to escape the noise and heat of the city, to read, to walk, to think, and to rest.

294
00:33:10,388 --> 00:33:20,320
A Roman writer named Pliny the Younger loved his country house near the sea, and he wrote letters describing it in loving detail.

295
00:33:20,320 --> 00:33:25,864
He wrote about its sunny rooms, its gardens, and the views of the water.

296
00:33:25,864 --> 00:33:35,180
And he described one small room, set a little apart from the rest of the house, where he could go to be completely alone.

297
00:33:35,180 --> 00:33:48,654
During the noisy midwinter festival, when the rest of the household was celebrating, he would retreat to that room, where he could hear none of the noise, and enjoy the quiet.

298
00:33:49,954 --> 00:33:53,647
There is a villa on the hillside now, below us.

299
00:33:53,647 --> 00:33:57,339
A few of its windows still glow with lamplight.

300
00:33:57,339 --> 00:34:01,503
Inside, the household is settling down for the night.

301
00:34:01,503 --> 00:34:04,253
The farm animals are in their pens.

302
00:34:04,253 --> 00:34:07,003
The dogs are curled up by the door.

303
00:34:07,003 --> 00:34:14,466
Out in the fields, the vines stand in long, patient rows, their leaves silver in the moonlight.

304
00:34:15,766 --> 00:34:19,875
The tall cypress trees stand still against the sky.

305
00:34:19,875 --> 00:34:34,134
A soft wind moves through the olive trees, and their leaves turn over, showing their pale undersides, so that the whole grove seems to shimmer gently, and then grows dark again.

306
00:34:35,434 --> 00:34:38,884
Somewhere in the valley, a nightingale is singing.

307
00:34:38,884 --> 00:34:41,162
The Romans loved the nightingale.

308
00:34:41,162 --> 00:34:56,067
Pliny the Elder, the uncle of Pliny the Younger, wrote in his great book about the natural world that in spring, when the leaves are thickening, the nightingale sings without stopping for many days and nights on end.

309
00:34:56,067 --> 00:35:01,449
Its song is long and varied, rising and falling, pausing, and beginning again.

310
00:35:01,449 --> 00:35:04,417
It is not singing for anyone in particular.

311
00:35:04,417 --> 00:35:06,901
It is simply singing into the night.

312
00:35:08,201 --> 00:35:14,012
Above the hills, the stars are very bright now, far brighter than in the city.

313
00:35:14,012 --> 00:35:17,066
The Romans looked up at these same stars.

314
00:35:17,066 --> 00:35:20,791
They gave them names, and told stories about them.

315
00:35:20,791 --> 00:35:32,115
They watched them turn slowly across the sky through the long night watches, and used them to tell the seasons, and to guide their ships across the sea.

316
00:35:33,415 --> 00:35:37,840
Those same stars are above you tonight, wherever you are.

317
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:51,425
They are turning slowly over your roof, just as they turned over the rooftops of Rome, over the temple of Vesta, over the harbour at Ostia, over this quiet villa in the hills.

318
00:35:51,425 --> 00:35:57,479
Two thousand years have passed, and yet the night sky is very nearly the same.

319
00:35:58,779 --> 00:36:02,011
In the villa, the last lamp is blown out.

320
00:36:02,011 --> 00:36:03,588
The windows go dark.

321
00:36:03,588 --> 00:36:06,032
The house settles into silence.

322
00:36:06,032 --> 00:36:11,550
The fields and the vineyards and the olive groves rest under the moon.

323
00:36:11,550 --> 00:36:21,009
And the long, quiet road we travelled earlier stretches away across the countryside, empty now, waiting for the morning.

324
00:36:22,309 --> 00:36:25,787
There is nowhere else you need to be tonight.

325
00:36:25,787 --> 00:36:27,951
Nothing else you need to do.

326
00:36:27,951 --> 00:36:36,453
You can let the hills hold you, and the stars watch over you, and the old stories carry on softly without you.

327
00:36:37,753 --> 00:36:42,578
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

328
00:36:42,578 --> 00:36:43,395
Rest well.

329
00:36:43,395 --> 00:36:44,459
Sleep deeply.

330
00:36:44,459 --> 00:36:45,603
And goodnight.
